## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of graph algorithms, state expansion techniques, and path reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm on expanded state space (node, used)
- Edge relaxation with and without discount application
- Path reconstruction across state transitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithDiscounts(n, edges, s, t, K) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>[]);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(null));

  const pq = new MinHeap();
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.insert([s,0,0]); // [node, used, distance]

  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [u,used,d] = pq.extractMin();
    if(d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if(u === t) break;

    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      // Without discount
      if(d + w < dist[v][used]){
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used,d+w]);
      }
      // With discount if available
      if(used < K && d < dist[v][used+1]){
        dist[v][used+1] = d;
        prev[v][used+1] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used+1,d]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Find optimal state at target
  let minDist = INF, minUsed = -1;
  for(let k=0;k<=K;k++){
    if(dist[t][k] < minDist){
      minDist = dist[t][k];
      minUsed = k;
    }
  }

  // Path reconstruction
  const path = [];
  let curr = [t,minUsed];
  while(curr && prev[curr[0]][curr[1]]){
    path.unshift(curr[0]);
    curr = prev[curr[0]][curr[1]];
  }
  if(curr[0] === s) path.unshift(s);

  return {distance: minDist, path};
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Unreachable target: return INF distance
- K=0: reduces to standard Dijkstra
- K larger than path length: effectively unlimited discounts
- Multiple optimal states: choose minimal used count

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formalize DP for partitioning into constrained blocks and compare top-down vs bottom-up implementations with proper modulo handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with pruning
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Positive number optimization
- Modulo arithmetic and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function countPartitions(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n) return 1;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];

    let sum = 0, ways = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum > target) break;
      if (sum === target) {
        ways = (ways + dfs(j + 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    return memo[i] = ways;
  }

  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulation sketch
function countPartitionsBottomUp(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n && sum + nums[j] <= target; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum === target) {
        dp[j+1] = (dp[j+1] + dp[i]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Empty input: return 0
- Large target: handle with modulo and pruning
- Single element equal to target: return 1
- No valid partition: return 0

## Correctness Justification
Top-down explores all valid partitions by recursively trying all possible block endings. Memoization avoids recomputation. Bottom-up builds from base case, accumulating ways to reach each position. Both respect positive numbers enabling early termination when sum exceeds target.

## Why This Is Asked

Standard quicksort performs well on average but can degrade to O(n²) on adversarial inputs, particularly with many duplicates. Introsort-style hybrids combine the practical efficiency of quicksort with worst-case guarantees, making-world engineering this a common real constraint.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)**: Partitions array into elements less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot, dramatically reducing comparisons for datasets with many duplicates
- **Recursion depth tracking**: Prevents stack overflow and performance degradation on pathological inputs
- **Hybrid switching**: Falls back to mergesort or heapsort when depth exceeds 2*log2(N) or partition size drops below a threshold (~16 elements)
- **Worst-case guarantees**: Ensures O(n log n) time complexity regardless of input distribution

## Code Example

